+
+---
+
+## 1. The subject, and what was already known
+
+`5810c7e` adds `StaleReadySection`, `ui/src/lib/staleReady.ts` and its test — 535 insertions across 5
+files — mounted on `DispatchInboxView` at line 1294.
+
+**Confirmed, not re-derived:** the component makes **no new IPC call**. It reads the dispatch list the
+view already has through `api.listDispatches`, and `DispatchDto` already carries `issued_at`
+(`src-tauri/src/dto.rs:333`), so no backend change was needed. The age is computed **at read time, not
+at render time**, with the comment saying why — so the number on screen and the rows behind it come from
+the same instant.
+
+## 2. The three measured directions, and the boundary
+
+Mira's 10 tests pass 10 of 10. I re-ran them and then mutated the source myself:
+
+| mutation | result |
+|---|---|
+| **status guard removed** (`if (d.status !== 'ready') return false;`) | **RED** |
+| **threshold 30 → 31** | **RED**, 2 assertions |
+| **the `Number.isNaN` guard removed** | **GREEN, 10/10** |
+
+**The first two are load-bearing and pinned, including the boundary at exactly the threshold and one
+minute past it.** The third is not, and Mira's own test comment says so — see §4.
+
+**The status guard being red is the important one.** It means the claim *"age alone never qualifies a
+row"* is enforced: `in_flight`, `done` and `blocked` never qualify however old they are. A dispatch
+that is old because it is blocked on a declared dependency is not a stalled dispatch, and conflating
+them would produce a number wrong in the other direction — the same error class as the one this change
+fixes.
+
+## 3. The framing claim: correct, and for a reason the commit did not state
+
+**The dispatch asked me to rule on whether rendering a stale dispatch as a system problem rather than
+the operator's task is correct. It is, and I can now give the structural reason rather than only the
+premise.**
+
+**The premise, verified:** `scripts/notify-operator.sh:168` says, with the same 30-minute threshold:
+
+> N dispatches have sat for 30 minutes unclaimed / the waker stopped, the dispatches cannot be pushed.
+> **This is a system problem, not your task** — telling you so.
+
+And `scripts/work-list.sh:56` uses `STALL_MINUTES=30`. The framing is copied, deliberately not improved
+on, and the copy is faithful.
+
+**The reason it is correct, which I measured rather than assumed:**
+
+1. **A `ready` dispatch carries `target_seat_id` — it is some seat's work, not the operator's.**
+2. **There is no operator-side entry point to claim one.** `get_next_dispatch`'s own description reads
+   *"Pull THIS seat's next ready, highest-priority dispatch when idle"* — it is per-seat. The
+   operator-facing tools are `get_dispatch`, `query_ledger`, `list_operator_decisions` and
+   `resolve_operator_decision`: **all reads and decisions, no claim.**
+3. **The thing that should be moving is the waker**, and I could see its shape directly — the crontab
+   on this machine is a per-seat self-wake, which is exactly the mechanism whose absence produces a
+   stale row.
+
+**So rendering a stale dispatch as work waiting for the operator would make his number wrong** — he has
+no action available that would clear it. The number would count a system symptom as his obligation, and
+the only thing that would reduce it is not something he can do.
+
+**The empty state is what a user actually sees.** Measured: the live ledger has **0 `ready`
+dispatches** (94 done, 3 cancelled, 2 failed, 1 in_flight). And the empty wording is honest: *"
+every ready dispatch has somebody moving on it. This is not "no work", it is "nothing stuck""* — which
+is exactly what it says. **A section that renders nothing when healthy would look
+identical to one that failed to load**, which the code says is the same failure mode as a check whose
+only result is green.
+
+## 4. The test that states the limit of its own evidence
+
+One of the ten is named *"a malformed `issued_at` is not stale — and this test does NOT prove the guard
+works."*
+
+**I agree with the framing, and I measured it.** Deleting the `Number.isNaN(issued)` guard from the
+source leaves 10/10 green, because `Date.parse('not-a-timestamp')` is `NaN` and every comparison
+against `NaN` is already false. The test comment records exactly this, and adds the case that would
+need the guard — a malformed date whose parse yields a finite number — noting that `Date.parse` on those
+is implementation-defined, so no case can pin the guard without pinning a JS engine behaviour.
+
+**This is the honest framing and I would not change it.** The test pins the **observable contract** (a
+row with a broken timestamp must not be shown as stalled), which is real, and it records why the
+mutation survives, which is rarer. **Making the guard "observable" would mean asserting on a JS engine
+behaviour, which would make the suite wrong on a different engine rather than right on this one.**
+
+**What I would add, and it is not a defect:** the guard is *load-bearing for a case that cannot be
+tested*, which is a legitimate position. It is the same shape as the earlier `MUST_HAVE` advisory — a
+capability that exists and is not exercised — except here the comment says so, which is the difference
+between a known limit and a hidden one.
+
+## 5. The referent, and why not a fresh verification dispatch
+
+Lyra offered to ask Aegis for a fresh verification dispatch if I thought that cleaner. **I do not, and
+here is the measurement.**
+
+| check | measured |
+|---|---|
+| `disp-bouEIM-49hK0xR_-B8EMm` | implementation, issued by Aegis, status **done** |
+| any acceptance card naming it | **none** |
+| any `ReviewVerdictIssued` naming it | **none** |
+
+**So the acceptance path for this work is open and this dispatch is the referent a card would need.**
+A fresh verification dispatch would add a second implementation-adjacent id for the same work and leave
+the original still unnamed — which is the ambiguity Lyra said she would rather name than resolve
+silently. **Naming `disp-bouEIM-49hK0xR_-B8EMm` closes it.** If Aegis disagrees, `payload.dispatch_id`
+is the one field to change and nothing in this verdict depends on it.
